# TLS Pie boot splash -- deliberately a plain black screen.
#
# plymouth's script language. Notes for anyone editing this, because the
# failure mode throughout is SILENCE: a syntax error, a missing image or a
# misspelled method gives you a black screen and nothing in any log. That used
# to be the tell that something had broken. IT IS NO LONGER: black is now the
# intended result, so if you are debugging this, check `--status` and the
# journal rather than reading the screen.
#
#   - variables are global unless declared local
#   - the refresh callback is invoked ~50 times a second
#   - there is no tiling. A scrolling background has to be two sprites.
#
# Preview it without rebooting:
#     sudo systemctl stop tls-kiosk
#     sudo plymouthd --mode=boot --tty=tty1 ; sudo plymouth show-splash
#     ... look at the panel ...
#     sudo plymouth quit ; sudo systemctl start tls-kiosk
#
# --- WHY THIS IS BLACK, 2026-08-12 ------------------------------------------
#
# It used to draw the Kizim artwork with a vertically-periodic rain layer
# scrolling down it, and the still was chosen to be the intro video's own first
# frame so the picture turned out to be where the video started.
#
# The operator asked for it to go. The boot sequence reads better without it:
# artwork, then a white flash, then the panel, then a video, then the panel
# again was four changes of subject before the rig was usable. Black -> video
# -> panel is one.
#
# ⛔ PLYMOUTH IS STILL DOING A JOB HERE, so do not conclude it can be removed.
# It is what holds the screen between the kernel handing over and cage mapping
# its first window. Without it that gap shows the getty LOGIN PROMPT and a
# blinking cursor -- which is exactly what all of this was built to cover. The
# artwork was the decoration; the covering is the function.
#
# The rain generator in tls_splash.py and its wrap-continuity tests are kept.
# They cost nothing when unused, and the property they check -- that the
# texture is periodic so the two sprites can swap invisibly -- is the sort of
# thing that is expensive to rediscover. To put the artwork back: restore the
# sprite blocks from git and re-add background.png / rain.png to setup_splash.sh.

# Nothing is drawn. plymouth clears to black, and black is the whole design.
#
# No refresh callback either: registering one to do nothing would wake this
# process 50 times a second through the entire boot for no pixels.
#
# Deliberately no progress bar, no spinner and no status text. The rig has one
# job at boot and the operator cannot act on "Starting user slice". Boot
# messages are still in the journal -- journalctl -b, and -b -1 for the previous
# boot now that it is persistent -- and real errors still print, because
# loglevel=3 keeps them.
#
# No password or question callbacks either: there is no encrypted volume here,
# and defining handlers for prompts that cannot occur is code that can only rot.
